{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c60d3f73-e22b-4438-86c8-353165ada787","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Uniform circular motion means constant speed around a circle. The radius locates the object relative to the center.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Uniform circular motion"},{"op":"figure","id":"orbit","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-2.7,3],"y":[-2.7,2.7],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"path","center":[0,0],"r":2},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0],"label":"O"},{"kind":"point","id":"object","at":[2,0]},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"object"},"stroke":"dashed","label":"r"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"speed","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"v=\\text{constant}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c60d3f73-e22b-4438-86c8-353165ada787","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Velocity is tangent to the circle. Although speed stays constant, velocity changes because its direction changes.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"velocity","on":"orbit","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"velvec","from":{"at":"object"},"to":[2,1.6],"arrow":true,"label":"v"},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"object"},"from":[2,1.6],"to":{"at":"center"},"r":0.12}]},{"op":"text","id":"direction","anchor":{"el":"speed","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"Changing direction means acceleration."}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c60d3f73-e22b-4438-86c8-353165ada787","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Acceleration points toward the center. Newton’s second law therefore requires an inward net force, called centripetal force—not an additional force.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"inward","on":"orbit","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"netvec","from":{"at":"object"},"to":[0.5,0],"arrow":true,"label":"Fnet"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"accel","anchor":{"el":"direction","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"a_c=\\frac{v^2}{r}"},{"op":"equation","id":"force","anchor":{"el":"accel","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"F_{\\text{net,inward}}=ma_c=\\frac{mv^2}{r}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If speed doubles at the same radius, what happens to the required inward net force?","expected_gist":"It quadruples because force is proportional to speed squared."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c60d3f73-e22b-4438-86c8-353165ada787","step":{"id":"s4","script":"For three kilograms moving at four meters per second around a two-meter radius, the required inward net force is twenty-four newtons.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"values","on":"orbit","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[2.35,1.85],"text":"4 m/s","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[1,-0.45],"text":"r = 2 m","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[2.2,-0.85],"text":"3 kg","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.9,0.5],"text":"24 N","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"force","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"F_{\\text{net,inward}}=\\frac{3(4)^2}{2}=24\\,\\mathrm{N}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
